ID: B2-Ch11a-Prophecy
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "As two old enemies clash one final time,

A great disaster that has once been avoided

Can no longer be prevented in the same way.

An entire continent lies in ruins,

While a single laugh made up from different voices

Echoes loudly throughout the realm."

The final syllable fades, and the revenant freezes in place once more, staring blankly into the distance.

A great disaster. Two old enemies. That matches the warning Eiden gave us about his clash with Tyrath. But the verse also mentioned a single laugh composed of multiple voices. I only know one person who sounds like that.

"Arraka..." I say. "That last line sounded uncomfortably familiar. You wouldn't happen to know anything about this disaster, would you?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "As two old enemies clash one final time,

A great disaster that has once been avoided

Can no longer be prevented in the same way.

An entire continent lies in ruins,

While a single laugh made up from different voices

Echoes loudly throughout the realm."

The final syllable fades, and Rose freezes in place once more, staring blankly into the distance.

A great disaster. Two old enemies. That matches the warning Eiden gave us about his clash with Tyrath. But the verse also mentioned a single laugh composed of multiple voices. I only know one person who sounds like that.

"Arraka..." I say. "That last line sounded uncomfortably familiar. You wouldn't happen to know anything about this disaster, would you?"

}
 "I could make that disaster happen before sunset if you like," Arraka says. "Save you the headache of puzzling it out. Just crack this amulet open. What do you say?"

"I'll figure it out on my own, thanks," I say.

"Your funeral," she replies.

"Is that all?..." Daren asks. "Is the prophecy finished?"

"If you're asking only about the verses, yes," Melindra says.

"Which means something else follows?" Daren asks.

"Only if this plays out like it did six centuries ago," Melindra says. "The last time I stood before a time weaver's prophecy, we didn't have to wait long before—"

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 The words cut off in her throat. An immense weight slams down through the clearing, crushing the air from our lungs. The sky itself shudders under an aura so massive and dense that the atmosphere turns to lead. As we struggle against the smothering pressure, the revenant lifts off the ground, drifting higher and higher, her yellow eyes fixed on empty space.

"What is this?!" Hadrik bellows. "Where is this magic coming from? Arraka's still locked up, isn't she?"

A chorus of laughter spills from the amulet.

"This isn't Arraka," Melindra says, straining to keep her voice steady. "I told you. That sage fight is nothing compared to what's coming."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 The words cut off in her throat. An immense weight slams down through the clearing, crushing the air from our lungs. The sky itself shudders under an aura so massive and dense that the atmosphere turns to lead. As we struggle against the smothering pressure, Rose lifts off the ground, drifting higher and higher, her yellow eyes fixed on empty space.

"What is this?!" Hadrik bellows. "Where is this magic coming from? Arraka's still locked up, isn't she?"

A chorus of laughter spills from the amulet.

"This isn't Arraka," Melindra says, straining to keep her voice steady. "I told you. That sage fight is nothing compared to what's coming."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Prophecy2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Prophecy2)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Prophecy2
TEXT:

 She isn't exaggerating. Not since Arraka broke free have I felt dread this sharp. Every nerve in my body screams to turn and sprint into the trees as fast as my legs can carry me. Giving in to panic now means instant obliteration. Who or what are we dealing with?

"Greetings, mortals..."

Several male voices speak as one, ringing through the forest from every direction at once. The layered cadence sounds identical to Arraka's chorus when her control slips. A laugh made of different voices... Is this the entity from the verse?

"You stand in the presence of a god," the voices boom, reverberating off the frozen pines. "Kneel before me, and you may gaze upon my magnificence."

"And who exactly—" Daren starts, but Melindra's hand slaps over his mouth, her eyes wide with terror.

"The God of Time," she whispers. "Don't question him. He can erase us in seconds. Just... kneel..."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 She drops to both knees, bowing low, and gestures urgently for Daren to follow.

Seeing Melindra yield so fast sends a cold chill down my spine. A wrong move here will get everyone killed. But bowing to this creature sticks in my throat.

Above us, the air condenses. A figure solidifies out of the light—human in shape and height, but sculpted from radiant, solid blue energy that hums with raw power. He has no face, no eyes, no mouth—only smooth blue radiance, with tiny azure flames flaring and dying across his skin at uneven intervals. He floats beside the hovering revenant, turning his blank countenance down toward us.

"It is unwise to keep a god waiting," the God of Time speaks. "I said kneel."

"Ignore him," Arraka's voice calls out from the amulet. "He needs you alive for his schemes. He can't touch you. He's bluffing."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 She drops to both knees, bowing low, and gestures urgently for Daren to follow.

Seeing Melindra yield so fast sends a cold chill down my spine. A wrong move here will get everyone killed. But bowing to this creature sticks in my throat.

Above us, the air condenses. A figure solidifies out of the light—human in shape and height, but sculpted from radiant, solid blue energy that hums with raw power. He has no face, no eyes, no mouth—only smooth blue radiance, with tiny azure flames flaring and dying across his skin at uneven intervals. He floats beside the hovering Rose, turning his blank countenance down toward us.

"It is unwise to keep a god waiting," the God of Time speaks. "I said kneel."

"Ignore him," Arraka's voice calls out from the amulet. "He needs you alive for his schemes. He can't touch you. He's bluffing."

}
choice("Kneel before the God of Time", B2-Ch11a-Kneel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Kneel, v_b2_ch11_kneel = 1)
choice("Stand my ground", B2-Ch11a-Kneel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Kneel, v_b2_ch11_kneel = 0)
choice("Stand my ground and insult the God of Time", B2-Ch11a-Kneel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Kneel, v_b2_ch11_kneel = 2)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Kneel
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_kneel == 1) {
 Arraka's reassurance carries zero weight. I drop to both knees beside Melindra, pressing my hands into the gravel.

"If you're waiting for me to bow," Daren says, planting his boots, "bring a chair. You'll be standing there a while."

An invisible blow caves in his breastplate. Metal shrieks, leather snaps, and Daren's heart tears clean out through the steel, suspended in mid-air in a spray of dark arterial blood.

"No—" Hadrik grunts, reaching out.

Daren's knees buckle. Before his body hits the earth, Hadrik's voice warps into a backwards slur. The blood snaps off the grass, the torn steel knits itself together, and the heart flies backward into Daren's chest without a seam. The reversal ends, and Daren drops forward onto his hands, chest heaving, drenched in cold sweat like a man dragged out of a drowning dream.

Laughter crackles from the gold amulet.

"Right," Arraka says. "I forgot he can rewind the clock while he holds the weaver. My mistake!"

"I will not speak a third time," the God of Time says. "Kneel."

Kate's jaw tightens. None of them move to obey.

"Sorry," Hadrik says, forcing a lopsided grin. "My doctor strictly forbade kneeling on account of my—"

"KNEEL!"

The command drops like an iron anvil. An overwhelming force slams us all flat against the jagged stone, crushing the air from my chest. Ribs crack. Blood pools in my throat as the sheer downward pressure grinds my organs against bone. Every ten seconds, the blue entity pulses, rewinding our agony just enough to stitch the ruptured flesh back together before crushing it anew.

Sight narrows to gray. Screams tear through the clearing—mine, Kate's, Daren's, all blurring into a single wall of noise. I clamp down, trying to turn numb, trying to let the agony pass through without tearing my mind apart. Every time I find a rhythm, another rewind jerks my consciousness back into the raw, fresh impact.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_kneel == 0) {
 "Arraka is right," I say, digging my heels in. "We don't bow to you. I'm standing right here."

A sickening tear rips through the left side of my chest. My ribs splinter outward, and a fist-sized void opens where my heart used to beat, pouring hot blood down my tunic.

"Barry!" Kate screams.

Warmth drains instantly from my limbs. A piercing frost spreads through my veins, and the clearing goes dark around the edges. Right as my vision blacks out, Kate's scream warps in reverse. The blood leaps back from the grass, the torn flesh seals shut, and my heart slams back into my ribs with the force of a hammer. I drop to my knees, gasping for air, shaking and soaked in sweat.

Arraka's laugh echoes from the casing.

"Right," she says. "I forgot he can rewind the clock while he holds the weaver. My mistake!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_kneel == 2) {
 "Kneel?" I say, looking straight at the floating shape. "To a glowing blue mannequin? Put some clothes on. Get a face. You're embarrassing both of us."

Pressure caves in my chest and ribs in a single crushing snap. Blood surges into my mouth, and an invisible fist rips my heart straight out through the torn muscle, leaving a smoking cavity.

"Barry!" Kate screams.

The world turns freezing cold. My knees hit the dirt, my vision tunneling toward black. Right before consciousness slips away, Kate's cry reverses into a garbled screech. The blood on my chin flows backward into my mouth, the chest wound knits shut, and my heart thumps back into place inside my ribs. I collapse forward, heaving air into raw lungs, shaking violently.

Arraka's laugh echoes from the casing.

"Right," she says. "I forgot he can rewind the clock while he holds the weaver. My mistake!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_kneel == 0 || v_b2_ch11_kneel == 2) {
 "I will not speak a third time," the God of Time says. "Kneel."

None of us move.

"Sorry," Hadrik says, forcing a lopsided grin. "My doctor strictly forbade kneeling on account of my—"

"KNEEL!"

The command drops like an iron anvil. An overwhelming force slams us all flat against the jagged stone, crushing the air from my chest. Ribs crack. Blood pools in my throat as the sheer downward pressure grinds my organs against bone. Every ten seconds, the blue entity pulses, rewinding our agony just enough to stitch the ruptured flesh back together before crushing it anew.

Sight narrows to gray. Screams tear through the clearing—mine, Kate's, Daren's, all blurring into a single wall of noise. I clamp down, trying to turn numb, trying to let the agony pass through without tearing my mind apart. Every time I find a rhythm, another rewind jerks my consciousness back into the raw, fresh impact.

}
 "Enough with the time rewinds!" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "You're making me dizzy!"

The crushing weight eases. The blue light pulses one last time to mend the bleeding organs, leaving us pinned face-down in the dirt under a firm, steady hold.

"Thank you," Arraka grumbles.

"Living among mortals has taught you some delayed manners, Arraka," the God of Time says. "A pity you lacked them before we cast you out of the magical plane."

"Cast me out?" Arraka snorts. "You ran because you were terrified. You fled during the banishment, and you ran again six hundred years ago. What happened to settling our duel, coward?"

"We will settle it," the god replies smoothly. "When both of us return to our peak. You hardly expect me to duel a golden trinket."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Absorb, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Absorb)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Absorb
TEXT:

 "Crush the trinket, then," Arraka says. "We can settle it right—"

"You receive no aid from me," the God of Time cuts in. "I have not forgotten the hundreds of spirits you butchered and absorbed. Many were comrades."

"Oh, don't pout, Selkram," Arraka mocks. "You owe me. If I hadn't invented spirit absorption, you never would have tried it yourself. You hid it from the other spirits, but you can't fool the creator of the craft. I know the side effects. Your voice split right after our first duel, and Ulruk suffered the exact same defect. Those extra voices leaking out of your throat are the souls you botched! I learned and adapted; you and Ulruk just fumbled through a stolen technique. You genuinely believed you could copy my life's work without practice? Then you blamed female spirits of the civilized races and banished us all rather than admit your own gross incompetence."

"I will not endure your—"

"You should have listened to Memphir," Arraka talks over him. "The only god with half a brain. He never touched absorption—I never heard his voice crack once. How is old Fate doing, by the way? Isn't his death prophecy set for this century?"

"The God of Fate is none of your—"

"It is this century," Arraka muses, thoroughly ignoring him. "Killed by an earthen mortal from a region named Varathia. Memphir checked that vision a thousand times, sweating over every scrying pool. Imagine his face when Eiden stamped the name Varathia onto this entire landmass! Now the great God of Fate crawls to Ulruk, trading favors just to concentrate massacres on this continent. Pathetic. If I were Memphir, I'd burn this entire landmass to cinder in an afternoon and be done with it."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Misery, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Misery)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Misery
TEXT:

 The God of Fate wants this continent wiped out as well. As if our odds were not grim enough.

"Arraka, silence yourself, or I will—" Selkram warns.

"You will what?" Arraka taunts. "Break the amulet?"

"I will fling you into the deepest trench in the ocean, never to be found."

"Try it," she laughs.

"Don't egg him on!" Illuna gasps from the dirt, her face pressed against the stone. "Throw that amulet in the sea and she breaks out on her own. It will destroy us all."

"Ah," Selkram says, turning his blank blue gaze down. "The jailor. Fitting that a banshee holds the leash, considering the misery her greed brought upon your kind."

"And your greed!" Flower shouts from the grass. "Petal never asked for this curse! She never wanted to kill, and neither did the others. I will never forgive you for what you did to them!"

"You will never forgive me?..." Selkram's chorus carries genuine amusement. "A fragile creature with a remarkably short memory. Let me refresh it."

He ramps the downward force over Flower. Her small frame grinds into the dirt, screams tearing from her throat. Before the bones can splinter entirely, a pulse of blue light rewinds the damage, only for the crushing pressure to slam down again.

"Oh, marvelous," Arraka groans. "More rewinds. Just shatter the amulet and end my misery."

choice("Try to get the God of Time's attention, so he'll focus on me, instead of Flower.", B2-Ch11a-Attention, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Attention)
choice("Leave the God of Time alone. He will surely stop by himself, eventually.", B2-Ch11a-Eventually, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Eventually)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Attention
TEXT:

 "Is this standard conduct for a god?" I call out, straining to lift my chin from the dirt. "Torturing children? Did dignity not make the cut on the magical plane?"

The blue pulse rolls back Flower's injuries. Selkram glides across the clearing, coming to a halt directly above me, his featureless azure face tilted downward.

"Fascinating," his layered voice speaks. "You possess virtually no magical aura, even by mortal standards. Yet you provoke me. What drives such folly?"

"Don't bother looking for logic in that one," Arraka chimes in. "He came at me with a steel dagger while I was fully unsealed. His survival instincts are broken. He's just a weakling—ignore him."

"He is not the only disappointment," Selkram says, rising back into the air to survey the group. "Aside from the stillwater girl, none of you compare to the group from six centuries ago. Back then, there was the golden fox, your stillwater host, and the monstrously powerful stillwater traveling at the fox's side. Eiden, if I recall. Compared to them, this gathering is a joke. Why the Magium directed the time weaver here is beyond me. You will likely perish within days."

"What are you..." I grunt against the soil. "You didn't send the weaver?"

"Ha!" Arraka mocks. "Selkram wishes he commanded time weavers. The Magium sent her, not this bloated blue phantom."

"The Magium?..."

"Standard practice," Arraka explains. "Whenever massive loss of life threatens this continent, the Magium dispatches a weaver. When their power awakens, they enter a trance, freeze time across the region, and walk to selected groups to deliver the verses. That has always been the ritual."

"Then where does he fit in?" I ask.

"He's a parasite," Arraka says. "Selkram learned long ago to latch onto a weaver's trance, piggybacking on the Magium link to insert his own trials. Other gods farm power from millions of worshipers. Selkram has zero followers, so he harvests high-yield targets—groups whose choices steer the fate of hundreds of thousands."

"What kind of trials?" I ask.

"They vary," Arraka says. "Though there is almost always a time-displacement stage involved. When does the main event start, Selkram?"

choice("Continue.", B2-Ch11a-Sanity, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Sanity) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
choice("Continue.", B2-Ch11a-Invitation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Invitation) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Eventually
TEXT:

 Antagonizing him further will only get someone killed. I keep my teeth clenched and stay down.

"Leave her alone!" Kate shouts, struggling against the crushing aura. "Haven't you done enough?!"

The blue pulse rolls back Flower's injuries. Selkram drifts over to Kate, hovering over her with his featureless face tilted down.

"Curious," the god says. "With such defiance in your tone, I expected a reserve of power to back it up. Yet you barely measure up to the stillwater in your company, let alone to me. Why invite destruction?"

"She's a weakling," Arraka says. "Don't waste your breath."

"She is not the only disappointment," Selkram says, rising back into the air to survey the group. "Aside from the stillwater girl, none of you compare to the group from six centuries ago. Back then, there was the golden fox, your stillwater host, and the monstrously powerful stillwater traveling at the fox's side. Eiden, if I recall. Compared to them, this gathering is a joke. Why the Magium directed the time weaver here is beyond me. You will likely perish within days."

"What are you..." I grunt against the soil. "You didn't send the weaver?"

"Ha!" Arraka mocks. "Selkram wishes he commanded time weavers. The Magium sent her, not this bloated blue phantom."

"The Magium?..."

"Standard practice," Arraka explains. "Whenever massive loss of life threatens this continent, the Magium dispatches a weaver. When their power awakens, they enter a trance, freeze time across the region, and walk to selected groups to deliver the verses. That has always been the ritual."

"Then where does he fit in?" I ask.

"He's a parasite," Arraka says. "Selkram learned long ago to latch onto a weaver's trance, piggybacking on the Magium link to insert his own trials. Other gods farm power from millions of worshipers. Selkram has zero followers, so he harvests high-yield targets—groups whose choices steer the fate of hundreds of thousands."

"What kind of trials?" I ask.

"They vary," Arraka says. "Though there is almost always a time-displacement stage involved. When does the main event start, Selkram?"

choice("Continue.", B2-Ch11a-Sanity, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Sanity) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
choice("Continue.", B2-Ch11a-Invitation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Invitation) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Sanity
TEXT:

 "As soon as preparations conclude," Selkram replies. "Though your time would be better spent in silence than conversing with vermin. Why indulge their questions? I doubt any of them will survive the opening stage."

Arraka's laugh rings clear.

"You're in for a surprise," she says. "These ones don't break easily."

"We shall see," Selkram says.

He drifts toward Rose, catching her chin between two glowing blue fingers. He turns her head from side to side, studying her inert face.

"Get your hands off her!" Flower screams from the ground, struggling against the crushing gravity.

"You care for her?" Selkram muses. "The time weaver is your friend... How convenient. She shall share in your trials, then. A companion in your agony. Let us draw her from the trance."

#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 1) {
 Selkram releases her chin and sweeps his hand across her brow. The yellow brilliance fades from Rose's eyes. She blinks, stumbling in mid-air beside the featureless blue phantom, looking around the frozen trees in sheer bewilderment.

"What is happening?..." she whispers. "How did I get here?"

"Your questions will find answers in the trial," Selkram tells her. "For now, know only this: you participate in a divine ritual. The only route to retaining your sanity lies in enduring what comes. We begin."

He pauses, projecting his voice across the canopy.

"To be entirely clear: this ritual claims every unbound soul within this reach of forest. Including whichever coward hides beneath an invisibility ward, watching from the thicket."

"Ha!" Arraka cackles. "Called it!"

"Commence," the god intones.

A searing white flash consumes the woods, swallowing every tree, stone, and face in pure blinding radiance.

}
#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 0) {
 Selkram releases her chin and sweeps his hand across her brow. The yellow brilliance fades from Rose's eyes. She blinks, stumbling in mid-air beside the featureless blue phantom, looking around the frozen trees in sheer bewilderment.

"What is happening?..." she whispers. "How did I get here?"

"Your questions will find answers in the trial," Selkram tells her. "For now, know only this: you participate in a divine ritual. The only route to retaining your sanity lies in enduring what comes. We begin."

Rose looks down, her eyes widening as she spots the rest of us pinned into the dirt.

"Oh, gods..." she gasps. "What happened to you?"

"Small misunderstanding with a god," I wheeze against the gravel. "Nothing to worry about."

"Prepare yourselves," the God of Time commands.

A searing white flash consumes the woods, swallowing every tree, stone, and face in pure blinding radiance.

}
choice("Continue.", B2-Ch11a-Cabin, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Cabin)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Invitation
TEXT:

 "As soon as preparations conclude," Selkram replies. "Though your time would be better spent in silence than conversing with vermin. Why indulge their questions? I doubt any of them will survive the opening stage."

Arraka's laugh rings clear.

"You're in for a surprise," she says. "These ones don't break easily."

"We shall see," Selkram says.

He drifts toward the revenant, catching her chin between two glowing blue fingers. He turns her head from side to side, studying her inert face.

"Traces of the God of Death linger in this weaver's core," Selkram observes. "A revenant. Or some crude offshoot of the undead. Regardless, she has earned her place in the trials. Let us wake her."

He releases her chin and passes a hand over her eyes. The golden light dissolves. The revenant blinks, surveying the clearing with flat, unfeeling composure before fixing her gaze directly on Selkram.

#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 1) {
 "You broke the trance," the revenant says, her voice level. "Am I to understand you intend to drag me into your ritual?"

"Perceptive," Selkram notes. "Ulruk trained you well."

"No training was required," she replies. "The binding spell implanted the God of Death's knowledge into my mind, including the nature of your trials and your temperament. Predicting your desire to punish Ulruk through his servant required very little deduction."

"Ulruk finally found a servant with more wit than himself," Selkram says. "Let us see if those wits preserve your mind in the crucible. Prepare yourself."

He pauses, projecting his voice across the canopy.

"To be entirely clear: this ritual claims every unbound soul within this reach of forest. Including whichever coward hides beneath an invisibility ward, watching from the thicket."

"Ha!" Arraka cackles. "Called it!"

"Commence," the god intones.

A searing white flash consumes the woods, swallowing every tree, stone, and face in pure blinding radiance.

}
