choice("Continue.", B2-Ch11a-Cabin, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Cabin)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Cabin
TEXT:

 Vertigo hits like a physical blow. My mind wrenches free of my skull, tumbling through empty space.

When the glare burns off, I am lying on damp earth, surrounded by tall grass. The others are gone.

I push myself up, scanning the treeline. The clearing, the slope of the ridge, the scent of damp pine needles—this is the valley forest where I grew up. Every tree stands exactly where I left it fifteen years ago.

I look down at my hands.

The fingers are small, short, and uncalloused. My sleeves hang loose, cut from rough homespun linen instead of my traveling tunic. My boots barely reach halfway up my shins.

Arraka's voice echoes in my memory: time displacement.

I slap my pockets. Empty. No throwing knives, no notebook, no stat device. Farther down the ridge, a dense, malevolent aura pulses into the air—a dark, suffocating vibration that belongs to only one creature. Thirteen years old again.

I take off running down the dirt path.

The cabin sits where the forest gives way to the clearing. Rough-hewn timber, an isolated patch of tilled earth, a hay barn, and the river rushing over gray stones behind the fence. My father built it far from the main village because he wanted quiet. I spent eight years hauling firewood through these exact pines.

At the edge of the trees, my lungs seize.

In the center of the front yard stands my little sister Claire. The banshee inside her is tearing its way to the surface. Her skin darkens to a bruised, mottled purple, her facial features stretching and melting as the possessing spirit violently reshapes her skeleton. The aura radiating from her frame is vile, freezing the marrow in my bones.

Near the barn, our donkey brays in terror, kicking against the wooden stall.

Down the path from the fields, three figures come into view: my mother Helen, my father Jonathan, and my younger brother, rushing toward the commotion.

They are alive. They are walking right into the yard.

The memory of the massacre flashes through my mind—the blood on the grass, the screams cut short, fifteen years of carrying their graves. I don't care about Selkram's trial. I don't care about rules. Here is a second chance to save my family.

choice("Attack the banshee right now, while she is still in the process of awakening", B2-Ch11a-Attack, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Attack)
choice("Wait for the banshee to attack my parents, before going in for the kill", B2-Ch11a-Wait, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Wait)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Attack
TEXT:

 Striking while the transformation is underway offers the only clean opening. Her senses will be sluggish.

I sprint into the barn, duck past the braying donkey, and snatch the iron pitchfork leaning against the hay bales.

A fleeting hesitation strikes: can she be talked down? No. That laugh still wakes me in cold sweats. This entity butchered my family for sport. She is not like Illuna. There is no bargaining with a freshly awakened monster.

I burst from the barn, weapon gripped in both hands. My parents and brother have already reached the yard, shouting in horror.

"Claire!" my mother screams. "Gods above, Claire!"

"Get a healer!" my father yells, voice cracking. "A magician—the kind Barry reads about! A mage! This is magic illness—if we find a doctor in town—"

"Father, get back!" I shout, running between them with the tines leveled.

"Barry?" he stares at the weapon. "What are you doing with that? Your sister is dying!"

"That isn't Claire anymore," I say, stepping past him. "She is a banshee. Move!"

"I don't care what you call it!" he snaps, planting himself firmly between me and the writhing figure. "You will not lay a finger on my daughter!"

"We don't have time—"

The transformation snaps shut. The banshee's eyes flare pitch-black. Exploiting our distraction, she lunges forward, driving her bare hand straight through my father's back. Her fingers punch clean through his chest, spraying hot blood across my face.

"No!" I scream as my father crumples, choking on his own blood.

I thrust the pitchfork with all my weight, but the creature is already moving. A jagged spike of solid stone erupts from the earth at her command, driving through my skull before the tines can even touch her skin.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Wait
TEXT:

 I sprint into the barn, duck past the braying donkey, and grab the pitchfork from the hay.

No hesitation. No foolish attempts to reason with a newly awakened banshee. This spirit revels in slaughter. It is kill or be killed.

I emerge into the yard as my parents rush up to Claire, trying desperately to reach whatever remains of their daughter.

"Claire!" my mother cries. "What is happening to you?!"

"We need a healer!" my father shouts in a panic. "A magician—the mages Barry talks about! There must be one in the next village. Barry, you're here! Is there a mage doctor nearby?!"

"There are no white mages in this entire region," I tell him, advancing with the pitchfork lowered. "And even if there were, it's already too late."

"What are you saying?..." my father demands. "Why are you holding that pitchfork?"

"Keep your eyes on Claire," I say, voice cold and steady. "She is turning into a monster. In five seconds, she will try to kill you."

"Claire? A monster?..." He looks back toward her, terrified. "Barry, this isn't a joke—"

"Watch her!"

The banshee's aura detonates outward. Her fingers hook into claws as she lunges for my father's throat. I shove him hard with my left shoulder, keeping the weapon braced in my right hand. Her talons miss his neck, gouging a bloody furrow across my left flank instead.

My mother gasps. "Barry!"

I drive the pitchfork forward with both hands, throwing all my body weight into the strike. The iron tines plunge through her ribs and pin her to the dirt. A hideous, high-pitched screech tears from her throat, thrashing violently against the tines before her limbs go limp and the dark aura collapses.

The iron clatters to the ground. My mother wraps her arms around me, sobbing into my shoulder.

"Thank you..." she whispers, trembling. "Thank you for saving your father. Losing both of you in one day would have killed me."

She pulls back abruptly, noticing the blood soaking my shirt.

"You're hurt. I'll get bandages and spirits from the pantry. It's a shallow cut—we can clean it. Stay right here!"

She sprints into the cabin. My father stands frozen, staring at the corpse, while my brother weeps near the fence.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Changed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Changed)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Changed
TEXT:

 It worked. I rewrote the past. If I have to live fifteen years all over again to reach twenty-eight, every single day will be worth it. My family is alive. As long as I can endure whatever trials Selkram throws—

A sudden violent dizziness shatters my train of thought. Pure white light floods the clearing, tearing my consciousness from my skull.

When the glare recedes, I am standing in the damp pine forest once more.

No. Not again.

I look down at my hands—thirteen-year-old palms, rough linen sleeves. Ahead, through the trees, the banshee's aura pulses with fresh, sickening power.

Every drop of blood spilled, every swing of the pitchfork, completely erased. The tragedy is resetting.

choice("Try to save my parents again.", B2-Ch11a-Daylight, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Daylight, v_b2_ch11_save_parents = 1)
choice("Let my parents die.", B2-Ch11a-Restrain, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Restrain, v_b2_ch11_save_parents = 0)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Daylight
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch11_hidden,1)
achievement("A hidden truth",v_ac_b2_ch11_hidden)
 I sprint for the barn, snatch the pitchfork from the straw, and charge straight across the yard. Before my parents can reach her, before the banshee can complete her transformation, I drive the iron tines through her chest with everything I have.

She collapses instantly into the dust.

No injuries. No hesitation. Killed before the dark aura could fully manifest. This has to be the answer. Why else would the ritual return me to this day?

"Barry..." my mother whispers, hands trembling at her mouth. "What have you done?"

The bruised purple skin fades from the corpse. The elongated bone snaps back into place, leaving only the lifeless body of an ordinary thirteen-year-old girl pinned to the dirt. My mother drops to her knees, gathering Claire's limp form into her arms, weeping hysterically.

My father stands over me, his knuckles white, venom burning in his eyes.

"You butchered her," he whispers. "Your own sister. Cold blood. Is this how you repay us for taking you off the street? For raising you under our roof?"

"Jonathan, stop!" my mother cries out.

"No, Helen!" he snaps, pointing a shaking finger at my chest. "He needs to hear it. I want him to know. Barry... remember every word while you rot in a cell. You are not our son. You never were. Your real parents were drifters who crept into our valley less than a year before you were born. A gang of cloaked thugs murdered them in broad daylight and vanished into the hills. We took you in because no one else would touch an orphan out of pity. Nobody wanted you. Nobody!"

A sharp crack cuts the air.

My mother's palm strikes my father's cheek, knocking his head aside.

"I said that is enough!" she shouts, tears streaming down her face.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Daylight2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Daylight2)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Restrain
TEXT:

 A cold realization settles in my gut. What if this trial isn't about saving them? What if Selkram sent me here to grind my face in my own helplessness? Arraka said these rituals harvest negative emotion. A god who takes pleasure in crushing ribs wouldn't offer an act of mercy out of goodwill.

The logic holds. Selkram did not forge time itself. He commands no power to rewrite history without a continuous spell. Unless a living caster sustains the reversal, the past remains fixed. The ritual demands the exact sequence of events from fifteen years ago.

I step back into the shadow of the pines, forcing my breathing steady. One more run through the nightmare, and the test concludes.

I wait thirty paces behind the treeline.

My parents' voices rise in confusion, followed by the wet crunch of stone tearing through bone and flesh. Screams tear through the yard—first my father, then my mother. Every instinct screams to break cover and drive the tines into the banshee's throat, but I force my boots into the dirt.

Silence falls over the yard.

I sprint from the treeline into the barn, seize the pitchfork, and round the corner. My younger brother is already fleeing across the grass, the banshee pursuing him with claws outstretched. With her back turned, she leaves herself wide open. I close the distance and ram the iron tines through her spine. She thrashes once, shrieking, and drops dead onto the gravel.

Beside her lie my parents' corpses. A dull ache tightens around my chest, but I push it down. They were already dead fifteen years ago. This is only a memory.

White light erupts from the soil. The vertigo returns, tearing my awareness loose once more.

When my vision clears, I am standing under the same pines, looking out at the yard as the dark aura pulses anew.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Daylight2
TEXT:

 My mother turns from my father and drops to her knees in front of me, gripping my shoulders.

"Barry, forgive him," she says, wiping tears from her cheeks with a rough sleeve. "The shock broke his temper. He didn't mean those words. We loved you from the moment we brought you home, blood or not."

She catches her breath, her eyes searching mine.

"And Claire... I know you wouldn't do this without cause. We felt that terrible aura rolling off her. We saw her face twisting into something monstrous before you struck. Your father and I know nothing of magic, but you read those books day and night. If that thing inside her was so dangerous that killing her was the only way to save the rest of us, I believe you. But swear it to me, Barry. Swear there was no other way."

"I swear it," I say. "There was no—"

Vertigo hits like a hammer. The world dissolves into blinding white radiance, tearing me backward through the void.

When the glare burns away, I am standing on the damp moss between the pines. Across the clearing, Claire's aura pulses with dark, volatile energy.

choice("Keep trying to save my parents ", B2-Ch11a-Trying, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Trying, v_b2_ch11_save_parents = 2)
choice("Let my parents die", B2-Ch11a-Spotted, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Spotted)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Trying
TEXT:

 Giving up now is impossible. My father lashed out because he watched his daughter die; my mother offered trust despite the horror. There has to be a way to save both of them.

I spend the next hour throwing myself into every conceivable combination. I strike with different tools, attack before my parents speak, attack after they intervene, attempt to talk the spirit down, and even put a scythe in my father's hands to strike her himself.

Every attempt ends in the same blinding white reset. Twice the banshee's stone spikes punch clean through my ribs, killing me on the spot, only for the loop to wrench me back to the pine trees. Selkram cares nothing for my survival—only for the loop itself.

The bitter truth takes root. The trial is not a puzzle with a secret solution. Selkram is an arrogant parasite using an old tragedy to milk despair. The past cannot be undone; the only way forward is to recreate the original timeline down to the last action.

I move through the woods toward the cabin, pacing my steps so I arrive late.

From the treeline, I listen as the banshee tears through my parents. The screams cut off one by one.

When the silence lands, I bolt into the barn, seize the pitchfork, and sprint into the yard. My brother runs for his life, the banshee hot on his heels. I flank her, ramming the iron tines through her ribs from behind. She screeches and collapses into the mud.

I stand over the corpses of my parents, chest burning with familiar grief.

The white light engulfs me once again. The vertigo tears through my skull.

When my vision clears, I am standing in the woods. Claire's aura pulses ahead.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Spotted
TEXT:

 The bitter truth takes root. The trial is not a puzzle with a secret solution. Selkram is an arrogant parasite using an old tragedy to milk despair. The past cannot be undone; the only way forward is to recreate the original timeline down to the last action.

I move through the woods toward the cabin, pacing my steps so I arrive late.

From the treeline, I listen as the banshee tears through my parents. The screams cut off one by one.

When the silence lands, I bolt into the barn, seize the pitchfork, and sprint into the yard. My brother runs for his life, the banshee hot on his heels. I flank her, ramming the iron tines through her ribs from behind. She screeches and collapses into the mud.

I stand over the corpses of my parents, chest burning with familiar grief.

The white light engulfs me once again. The vertigo tears through my skull.

When my vision clears, I am standing in the woods. Claire's aura pulses ahead.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Pitchfork
TEXT:

 No. This makes no sense. I let both parents die. I protected my brother. I struck with the pitchfork from behind. I arrived late, exactly as I remembered...

Unless my memory is a lie.

The story I told everyone for fifteen years—the neat, comfortable excuse that I arrived too late to do anything—shatters into dust. I told it so many times that I came to believe it myself. But the ritual does not accept lies.

The real sequence floods back. Shock, cold resignation, and a blinding surge of hatred for Selkram.

I walk out of the trees, enter the barn, and lift the pitchfork. Then I step into the open yard and stop twenty paces back, holding the weapon at my side while my parents plead with the creature.

The awakening snaps shut. The banshee lunges for my father. He sidesteps the first swipe, but she summons a jagged wedge of rock, driving it straight through his thigh. He collapses, howling in agony. The strain of the casting makes the banshee shriek in pain, clutching her head.

My father looks up and catches sight of me standing frozen with the pitchfork.

"Barry..." he gasps, reaching out. "Throw it! Throw the pitchfork!"

I stand motionless, staring through him, the rage against the God of Time boiling behind my teeth.

"Barry!" he screams in terror. "Throw it!"

The banshee recovers. A second stone spike punches straight through his breastbone, killing him instantly.

My mother's scream pierces the clearing. She turns toward me, paralyzed with terror.

"Barry... please..."

I do not move. A stone blade slices across her throat, and she collapses beside my father.

The banshee turns and looks at me standing frozen over the bodies. A ragged, high-pitched laugh spills from her mouth. The sinister cackle that has haunted my sleep for fifteen years.

She wasn't laughing at her kill. She was laughing at me.

I didn't arrive late. I stood right there with a weapon in my hands, paralyzed by sheer, unadulterated terror. I listened to my father and mother beg for help and could not make my limbs move an inch. Only when the monster turned toward my little brother did my body break the freeze. That was the truth I buried for half my life.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Eliminated, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Eliminated)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Eliminated
TEXT:

 That day in the yard was the only time in my life I ever froze in terror. After that massacre, something broke inside my head. The horror of freezing up again erased my self-preservation entirely, leaving behind the reckless, suicidal drive that has pushed me into danger ever since. Fifteen years of buried shame, unearthed by a divine parasite. I'd offer Selkram my thanks, but I'd rather break his neck.

The banshee turns toward my brother.

I break into a dead sprint, driving the pitchfork through her back before she can take a step. The tines pierce her heart, and she collapses lifeless onto the gravel.

White light detonates across the yard. The pull on my consciousness is instantaneous, but the panic is gone. I passed.

The glare burns away. I am back in my twenty-eight-year-old frame, surrounded by the frozen trees of Varathia.

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "First one out of the gate, Barry-boy!" Arraka cackles. "Grab a seat. The show is just getting good for the rest of your friends!"

I take in the clearing. Selkram is nowhere to be seen, but the ritual holds firm. Daren, Kate, Leila, Hadrik, and the revenant stand rigid, eyes blazing with bright yellow light, locked in their trances. Melindra, Illuna, and Flower have already surfaced, watching me closely.

"You can watch what they're seeing?" I ask, catching my breath.

"Of course," Arraka says. "They are tethered directly to the Magium right now. Tap the aura stream and you see whatever their minds project."

"The Magium..." The conversation before the trial comes back to me. "You said the Magium dispatched the revenant with the prophecy. Is the Magium a living person?"

Arraka howls with laughter. "Illuna, did you hear that? He asked if the Magium is a person!"

"I heard," Illuna replies, completely flat.

"Fine, keep your secrets," I say, glancing at the amulet. "What about the others' trials?"

"The ones who finished, or the ones still suffering?" Arraka asks.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "First one out of the gate, Barry-boy!" Arraka cackles. "Grab a seat. The show is just getting good for the rest of your friends!"

I take in the clearing. Selkram is nowhere to be seen, but the ritual holds firm. Daren, Kate, Leila, Hadrik, and Rose stand rigid, eyes blazing with bright yellow light, locked in their trances. Melindra, Illuna, and Flower have already surfaced, watching me closely.

"You can watch what they're seeing?" I ask, catching my breath.

"Of course," Arraka says. "They are tethered directly to the Magium right now. Tap the aura stream and you see whatever their minds project."

"The Magium..." The conversation before the trial comes back to me. "You said the Magium dispatched Rose with the prophecy. Is the Magium a living person?"

Arraka howls with laughter. "Illuna, did you hear that? He asked if the Magium is a person!"

"I heard," Illuna replies, completely flat.

"Fine, keep your secrets," I say, glancing at the amulet. "What about the others' trials?"

"The ones who finished, or the ones still suffering?" Arraka asks.

}
choice(""Both, I guess?..."", B2-Ch11a-Transparent, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Transparent, v_b2_ch11_both = 1)
choice(""The ones that are still inside their rituals, obviously."", B2-Ch11a-Transparent, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Transparent, v_b2_ch11_both = 0)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Transparent
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_both == 1 && v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "Both, I guess?..."

"Well," Arraka says, "I missed Melindra's run, but Illuna was forced to replay the sacred woods massacre down to the last drop of blood. Her trial lacked comedic value because she figured out the win condition immediately, though she was furious beneath that icy face. Flower, meanwhile, looped through trying to rescue her circus friends from Olmnar's fire dozens of times in a row. Illuna had to pull her out after finishing her own trial."

"What about the ones still inside?" I ask.

"Quicker to show you," Arraka says. "Let's start with our righteous healer."

The clearing dissolves in a flash.

The forest vanishes, replaced by the sunlit stone courtyard of Rose's estate in Thilias. Flower and I are translucent, our hands and bodies ghostly and faint, along with the gold amulet on her chest. Across the yard, solid, physical versions of past-me and past-Flower stand talking with Rose and her siblings. Solid Hadrik, Kate, and Daren stand nearby, with Ella standing beside the porch.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_both == 1 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "Both, I guess?..."

"Well," Arraka says, "I missed Melindra's run, but Illuna was forced to replay the sacred woods massacre down to the last drop of blood. Her trial lacked comedic value because she figured out the win condition immediately, though she was furious beneath that icy face. Flower, meanwhile, looped through trying to rescue her circus friends from Olmnar's fire dozens of times in a row. Illuna had to pull her out after finishing her own trial."

"What about the ones still inside?" I ask.

"Quicker to show you," Arraka says. "Let's start with our righteous healer."

The clearing dissolves in a flash.

The forest vanishes, replaced by the sunlit stone courtyard of Rose's estate in Thilias. Flower and I are translucent, our hands and bodies ghostly and faint, along with the gold amulet on her chest. Across the yard, solid, physical versions of past-me and past-Flower stand talking with Rose and her siblings. Solid Hadrik, Kate, and Daren stand nearby in silence.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_both == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "The ones that are still inside their rituals, obviously."

"Quicker to show you," Arraka says. "Let's start with our righteous healer."

The clearing dissolves in a flash.

The forest vanishes, replaced by the sunlit stone courtyard of Rose's estate in Thilias. Flower and I are translucent, our hands and bodies ghostly and faint, along with the gold amulet on her chest. Across the yard, solid, physical versions of past-me and past-Flower stand talking with Rose and her siblings. Solid Hadrik, Kate, and Daren stand nearby, with Ella standing beside the porch.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_both == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "The ones that are still inside their rituals, obviously."

"Quicker to show you," Arraka says. "Let's start with our righteous healer."

The clearing dissolves in a flash.

The forest vanishes, replaced by the sunlit stone courtyard of Rose's estate in Thilias. Flower and I are translucent, our hands and bodies ghostly and faint, along with the gold amulet on her chest. Across the yard, solid, physical versions of past-me and past-Flower stand talking with Rose and her siblings. Solid Hadrik, Kate, and Daren stand nearby in silence.

}
 "Context first," Arraka says.

"How did you drag us into this?" I ask.

"Quiet," she cuts in.

Across the yard, past-Daren speaks to Rose: "Could you show me to my quarters? I need to lie down."

"Right this way," Rose says, leading him inside.

"The healer retreats to brood," Arraka narrates. "Remember this?"

"Yes," I say. "Right after we reached the mansion. What does this have to do with his trial?"

"Patience," Arraka says. "Skipping ahead to the bedroom."

The courtyard melts away. We stand in Daren's guest quarters, where his solid past self sits slumped on the edge of the mattress, elbows on knees, burying his face in his hands.

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "He can't hear or see us," Arraka clarifies. "We're spectating."

"She could have died..." Daren's voice reverberates from the walls, though his lips never part. "Right before my eyes, and I was powerless to stop it..."

"What is that?" I look around the room. "His thoughts?"

"Of course his thoughts," Arraka says.

"You can read minds?"

"Don't be dense," she snaps. "They are hooked directly into the Magium. Reading an active surface thought in this state is basic aura mechanics. Shut up and listen."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "He can't hear or see us," Arraka clarifies. "We're spectating."

"She's dead..." Daren's voice reverberates from the walls, though his lips never part. "Butchered right in front of me, and I was powerless to save her..."

"What is that?" I look around the room. "His thoughts?"

"Of course his thoughts," Arraka says.

"You can read minds?"

"Don't be dense," she snaps. "They are hooked directly into the Magium. Reading an active surface thought in this state is basic aura mechanics. Shut up and listen."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Luggage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Luggage)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Luggage
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "No..." Daren's voice echoes from the plaster. "I wasn't powerless. The power was offered, and I refused. Could I have stomached asking Eiden for help to save her? What if Barry hadn't stepped in? Would that girl have died because of my pride? Eiden is malicious—he never offers gifts without a hook. But he asked for nothing in return. He gave Barry the bypass, and the rest of us received it as fallout... Why? Did he offer it purely to humiliate me? What is he playing at?..."

"There," Arraka says. "That sets the stage. Moving to the main event."

The bedroom dissolves. The bustling cobblestones of Thilias materialize around us. The Earl of Ollendor stands over Ella, blade drawn. Flower and I hover in our translucent forms a dozen paces away, while past-me, Daren, Kate, Rose, and Eiden watch from the street.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "No..." Daren's voice echoes from the plaster. "I wasn't powerless. The power was offered, and I let pride get in the way. I believed I could save her through sheer willpower, like always. Even when the noble drew steel, I refused to see reality. I let that girl die because I couldn't stomach begging Eiden. His terms were clear—admit weakness, nothing more. And I failed. Then I slaughtered the noble for empty revenge, putting everyone in danger. What good are decades of training if I can't protect one person standing right in front of me?..."

"There," Arraka says. "That sets the stage. Moving to the main event."

The bedroom dissolves. The bustling cobblestones of Thilias materialize around us. The Earl of Ollendor stands over the slave girl, blade drawn. Flower and I hover in our translucent forms a dozen paces away, while past-me, Daren, Kate, Rose, and Eiden watch from the street.

}
 "This is where Daren's trial begins," Arraka says. "Clear on the stakes?"

"Clear enough," I say.

"Watch the show."

"Where am I?..." Daren's thoughts ring out as he blinks at the street. "The trial? Back in Thilias?"

"Fascinating," Eiden speaks up, eyes shut and hands tucked in his pockets. "The healer's aura shifted entirely in an instant. Like a different man stepped into the body. What causes such a disruption... memory loss? No. A time trial from the God of Time?"

"I don't know what you mean," Daren attempts to bluff. "I've never heard of such a god."

"A trial it is," Eiden smirks. "And the objective? Saving the slave? In that case, I shall refrain from interfering. I wouldn't want you failing on my account."

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "Refrain?" Daren's voice sharpens with panic. "You're still giving Barry the power to strike the noble, aren't you?"

"Is that how it played out originally?" Eiden's smile turns sharp. "Barry saved her because you refused my terms, did he? Unfortunately, your companion won't clean up your mess this time. What will you do, healer?"

"Bastard..." Daren thinks. "If Barry can't strike..."

Down the street, Ella cries out as the Earl draws his hunting dagger.

"Please!" she weeps, clutching the oversized travel chests. "One more chance! I can carry them! Please don't kill me!"

"You should have considered that before wasting my afternoon, wench," the noble sneers, closing the distance. "Hold still. I'll make it quick."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "Refrain?" Daren's voice sharpens with panic.

"You understand perfectly," Eiden smiles. "The offer I made in your original timeline is withdrawn. You are on your own. What will you do, healer?"

"Bastard..." Daren thinks. "No power..."

Down the street, the slave girl cries out as the Earl draws his hunting dagger.

"Please!" she weeps, clutching the oversized travel chests. "One more chance! I can carry them! Please don't kill me!"

"You should have considered that before wasting my afternoon, wench," the noble sneers, closing the distance. "Hold still. I'll make it quick."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Tightly, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Tightly)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Tightly
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 Daren charges forward, grabbing Ella by the wrist.

"Come, Ella," he urges. "I'm pulling you out of here."

"No!" Ella screams, yanking her arm back in sheer panic. "I can't run! I am his property!"

She turns back toward the nobleman. "My lord, please, I—"

The dagger flashes. The blade draws a clean red line across her throat.

"No!" Daren shouts, catching her as she falls. White healing light flares from his palms, flooding the wound.

Too late. The severed artery drains her in seconds. The light flickers out over a lifeless body.

"Pathetic," the Earl sneers, wiping his blade.

"No, this is wrong!" Daren weeps, clutching Ella's body to his breastplate. "She was supposed to live at Rose's home. She was safe! Please... whoever commands this trial... give me one more chance. I won't fail her. Please..."

White light engulfs the street.

When the radiance fades, Ella is alive again, struggling with the luggage, and Daren stands back at the start of the street.

"Ahahaha!" Arraka cackles. "Look at the timing! Pure theater!"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 Daren charges forward, grabbing the slave girl by the wrist.

"Come with me," he urges. "I'm pulling you out of here."

"No!" the girl screams, yanking her arm back in sheer panic. "I can't run! I am his property!"

She turns back toward the nobleman. "My lord, please, I—"

The dagger flashes. The blade draws a clean red line across her throat.

"No!" Daren shouts, catching her as she falls. White healing light flares from his palms, flooding the wound.

Too late. The severed artery drains her in seconds. The light flickers out over a lifeless body.

"Pathetic," the Earl sneers, wiping his blade.

"No, not again!" Daren weeps, clutching the corpse to his breastplate. "I was going to save you! I was going to make it right! Please... give me one more chance. Please..."

White light engulfs the street.

When the radiance fades, the slave girl is alive again, struggling with the luggage, and Daren stands back at the start of the street.

"Ahahaha!" Arraka cackles. "Look at the timing! Pure theater!"

}
choice(""Just what is it, exactly, that you are finding so funny?"", B2-Ch11a-Funny, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Funny)
choice(""Is this seriously what passes off as entertainment to you?"", B2-Ch11a-Entertainment, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Entertainment)
choice(""You really are starting to get on my nerves, you know that?"", B2-Ch11a-Nerves, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Nerves)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Funny
TEXT:

 "Just what is it, exactly, that you are finding so funny?"

"Aside from the obvious agony?" Arraka says. "The oaf genuinely thinks his prayer bought him another attempt. Selkram couldn't care less whether that slave breathes or dies."

"Saving her isn't the win condition?" I ask.

"Of course not," Arraka says. "You went through your own trial—you know how this works."

In the street, Eiden reads Daren's aura reset for the second time.

"I must refrain from interfering," Eiden repeats with a smile.

"No!" Daren pleads, desperation cracking his voice. "I need your power! The power to strike the noble!"

"Unusual candor," Eiden muses. "Yet solving your trial with borrowed magic defeats the purpose. I decline."

"Damn you!" Daren shouts. "Do you want me to beg?!"

"Actually, I was—"

Daren drops hard onto his knees, planting his palms into the cobblestones and bowing his head to the dirt.

"Then I beg!" Daren shouts into the dust. "On my hands and knees! I despise you. I consider you vile and irredeemable. But you are the only power that can save this girl from slaughter. I will do anything you ask. Anything. Just give me the strength to save her!"

Eiden's eyelids snap open, wide with genuine shock. He stares down at the prostrate knight for several seconds. Then his eyes drift shut, a slight smile touching his lips.

"Anything, you say?" Eiden's tone turns deadly quiet. "Then I have one demand."

He looks toward the healer.

"Prove me wrong."

Wind roars across the cobblestones, swirling in a tight vortex around the stillwater.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Destination, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Destination)


