choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Destination, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Destination)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Destination
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "Prove you wrong?" Daren asks, blinking as the swirling wind dies down. "What does that—"

"You have the power, healer," Eiden cuts in smoothly. "Run along before she bleeds out."

Daren scrambles up from the cobblestones and charges toward the alley.

Across the street, the Earl of Ollendor taps the flat of his dagger against his palm. "How many reprieves do you expect, wench? My patience is not endless. Hold still."

"Get away from her!" Daren bellows, drawing his broadsword in a sweeping arc.

The Earl wheels around. "What in the—"

Steel shears through vertebrae. The nobleman's head flies into the gutter, followed an instant later by his headless corpse slumping into the dirt.

"I did it..." Daren's thoughts ring out, breathless with relief. "She's alive. I didn't hesitate."

White light detonates from the pavement, blinding and instantaneous.

"No—wait!" Daren screams as the radiance claims him.

When the glare recedes, the Earl stands whole and breathing over Ella, dagger poised. Daren stands thirty paces back at the entrance of the street.

"Ahahaha!" Arraka howls. "He actually thought he passed! All the fool needs to do is steady his pulse and keep his mouth shut so Eiden doesn't read the temporal resonance in his aura. At this rate, he'll loop until the next century!"

"Does his trial mirror mine?" I ask. "Recreate the original events?"

"Most of them work the same way," Arraka says. "Once your past self uses Eiden's power to handle the noble, the loop will release him. Let's check on the others."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "Prove you wrong?" Daren asks, blinking as the swirling wind dies down. "What does that—"

"You have the power, healer," Eiden cuts in smoothly. "Run along before she bleeds out."

Daren scrambles up from the cobblestones and charges toward the alley.

Across the street, the Earl of Ollendor taps the flat of his dagger against his palm. "How many reprieves do you expect, wench? My patience is not endless. Hold still."

"Get away from her!" Daren bellows, drawing his broadsword in a sweeping arc.

The Earl wheels around. "What in the—"

Steel shears through vertebrae. The nobleman's head flies into the gutter, followed an instant later by his headless corpse slumping into the dirt.

"I did it..." Daren's thoughts ring out, breathless with relief. "She's alive. I didn't hesitate."

White light detonates from the pavement, blinding and instantaneous.

"No—wait!" Daren screams as the radiance claims him.

When the glare recedes, the Earl stands whole and breathing over the slave girl, dagger poised. Daren stands thirty paces back at the entrance of the street.

"Ahahaha!" Arraka howls. "He actually thought he passed! Imagine his face when he realizes the slave has to die for the trial to release him!"

"Does his trial mirror mine?" I ask. "Recreate the original events?"

"Most of them work the same way," Arraka says. "First he needs to rein in his aura so Eiden doesn't spot the anomaly. Then he must refuse Eiden's offer and let the sequence play out to the girl's death. Let's check on the others."

}
 "What went wrong?..." Daren's thoughts echo from the street below. "What does this ritual want from me?!"

"Next stop, the Beacon of Hope!" Arraka announces. "Brace yourselves—and we're already here."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Underground, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Underground)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Underground
TEXT:

 Cold, stagnant air hits my face. We stand in a damp subterranean tunnel of smoothed stone, lit by sputtering tallow torches spaced every twenty paces.

The screaming starts almost instantly.

Farther down the hall, a young boy's voice cracks in a high, ragged shriek. Closer by, a girl's scream echoes off the stone, raw and breathless with pain. Within seconds, a dozen different children's voices join the chorus—some begging for mercy, some sobbing for parents who will never come, others muffled behind thick iron doors.

Kate and Leila described this facility, but the reality turns my stomach. The agony bouncing off these walls makes the hair on my arms stand straight up.

"The Lessathi certainly keep busy," Arraka remarks. "A full chorus of misery. Hold on while I locate Kate's current loop so we can skip the boring setup. Enjoy the ambient noise while I work."

"These are children," I snap at the casing. "What is wrong with you?"

"Physical pain bores me," Arraka replies smoothly. "Psychological torment is the real art form. Watching your face twist while you listen to them—now that is entertainment. Why else would I drag you along?"

choice(""I thought you were just bored, and decided to bring me along on a whim."", B2-Ch11a-Bored, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Bored)
choice(""I still don't understand how you can find entertainment in such things..."", B2-Ch11a-Understand, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Understand)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Bored
TEXT:

 "I thought you were just bored, and decided to bring me along on a whim."

"Hardly," Arraka says. "That's Eiden's habit. I rarely act without purpose. I picked this exact hour because the experimentation block runs at full capacity now. Leaving you in an empty corridor would have wasted a prime opportunity."

"Get us out of this hallway," I say.

"Patience," she replies. "Even for a genius of my caliber, sorting through temporal memory streams takes a moment. Connecting directly to the Magium isn't a daily routine."

"You drop the Magium's name every five minutes without explaining what it actually is," I point out.

"That's the point, Barry-boy. Your frustration keeps me amused while I sift through these timelines."

"How are you shifting between past and future so easily?" I ask. "And how are we standing inside other people's memories?"

"Tethered to the Magium as we are, concepts like past, future, and memory lose their rigid borders," Arraka says. "Everything is simultaneously real and imagined. Observe."

A crimson winged serpent wreathed in roaring fire materializes out of thin air, lunging straight for my chest with bared fangs. I stumble backward, reaching for a blade that isn't on my belt. The creature's claws rake across my right thigh before it dissolves into smoke.

"Arraka, stop it!" Flower gasps, stepping forward as Illuna raises her hands to weave healing magic.

"Relax," Arraka says. "Did the scratch burn, Barry?"

I look down at my leg. The fabric is unblemished; the skin beneath is intact. Illuna lowers her hands, stepping back.

"Not a scratch," I admit.

"Because we are astral projections," Arraka says. "Our physical shells remain pinned in the forest with yellow eyes. Within this tether, my authority is broad. I cannot alter your friends' choices in their active loops, but my spectating access is unrestricted."

"So my friends aren't in danger of dying either?" I ask. "Their trials are illusions?"

"No," Arraka corrects. "That rule applies only to our projection. The realms your companions occupy are entirely physical parallel timelines branch-locked from the moment of divergence. Changes there won't rewrite our timeline, but the danger to their minds is absolute. Did you think your trial was forgiving? That was Selkram manipulating the outcome to wring out despair."

"Selkram..." The name tastes like bile. "Is he watching us right now?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11a-Materialized, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11a-Materialized)


ID: B2-Ch11a-Understand
TEXT:

 "I still don't understand how you can find entertainment in such things..."

"Give me some grace," Arraka sighs. "I might have turned out normal too if I hadn't watched my parents butchered before my eyes as an innocent child."

"Really?..." I say.

"Of course not, moron! Banshees don't have parents. Do you take me for Flower?"

"Get us out of this hallway," I say.

"Patience," she replies. "Even for a genius of my caliber, sorting through temporal memory streams takes a moment. Connecting directly to the Magium isn't a daily routine."

